<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aksumite Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aksum, Kingdom of Aksum, Aksumites, Aksumite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Aksumite Empire was a trading state centred on Aksum in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-highlands">Ethiopian highlands</a> and present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, flourishing from roughly the first to the seventh century CE. Through the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adulis">Adulis</a> it traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, minted its own coins and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana">King Ezana</a>. It is regarded as an ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> and contributed to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire.jpg" alt="Aksumite Empire" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KingEndybisEthiopia227-235CE.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its influence stretched over the Nuba Mountains, the Butana, the Gash Delta, where a vassal king ruled, and, by the accounts of Ibn Hawqal and al-Aswani, the Eastern Desert to the Red Sea, lands once under <b>Aksum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alodia">Alodia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Succession is poorly documented, but the throne seems to have passed mostly within the ruling family until the crises of the early sixth century, when <b>Aksum</b> intervened to impose kings of its own choosing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first three centuries CE Saba fought long wars with Himyar, Hadramawt and the <b>Kingdom of Aksum</b>, whose troops intervened in western Yemen, and kings such as Ilsharah Yahdub and Sha’irum Awtar recorded their campaigns in dedications at the Awwam temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Externally, the rise of the <b>Kingdom of Aksum</b> posed a growing threat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom of <b>Aksum</b> in Ethiopia became Christian in the 4th century.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Ge'ez was the language of the <b>Kingdom of Aksum</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/geez">Ge'ez · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
